Distribution test for comms_allgather_kblocks.

The routine assembles an array indexed by the whole k-point set out of the blocks each rank
owns. Every k-point belongs to exactly one rank, so the assembly is a copy: this test
therefore demands bit-exact equality rather than a tolerance, and it compares on every rank
rather than only on the root, because the routine is expected to leave all ranks holding the
same array. The global array is pre-filled with a sentinel, so a block the routine fails to
write counts as a failure instead of passing as a plausible value.

Nine distributions are covered, at three mesh sizes derived from the rank count:

  1 contiguous    blocks in rank order, what comms_array_split produces
  2 unequal       contiguous, but the ranks own different numbers of k-points
  3 empty-rank    contiguous, with one rank owning nothing at all
  4 reversed      contiguous blocks handed out in reverse rank order
  5 round-robin   strided, so nothing is consecutive anywhere
  6 one-swap      contiguous but for a single pair of k-points exchanged
  7 gap           every rank consecutive, but one k-point owned by nobody
  8 malformed     a local leading dimension that disagrees with the global array
  9 malformed     more local blocks than global_k accounts for

Both the number of wrong elements and the error flag are reduced over the ranks, so a
failure on a rank that does not print is still counted. A rank that owns no k-point is
handed a zero-sized block, which is what its shape has to say: padding it to one is a
shape mismatch, and a guard that fires on one rank alone leaves the others waiting in the
collective rather than returning an error.

Cases 1 to 7 must assemble the array exactly. Cases 8 and 9 must raise an error rather than
assemble anything.

Two of them are there for reasons that are easy to miss. Case 6 is contiguous apart from one
exchanged pair: a routine that mistook it for the identity would write the blocks at the
wrong offsets and corrupt the array without raising anything. Cases 1 to 3 matter in the
other direction -- they must not be rejected or handled by the slow path, because a check
that was merely over-cautious would still produce correct results and no comparison of
output could reveal it.

Which of the routine's internal paths a given distribution takes is deliberately not
asserted. It is not observable from outside and it is not part of the contract; what is
asserted is that every distribution assembles the same correct array.

One mesh size equals the rank count, so that each rank owns exactly one k-point. Several of
the distributions collapse onto the identity there, which is worth covering precisely because
it is the case where a test can accidentally expect the wrong thing.

Built without MPI the routine's serial branch reduces the assembly to a local copy; this
test is registered only in MPI builds, so that branch is covered by the rest of the suite.
